on my channel "Firm Learning."

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.730
Today, I'm back in my flat in Munich.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.280
We got a new balcony

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.480
and I spent some time building
the new furniture today

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.480
but now I'm back and creating
today's video for you.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.970
And today I'm talking about
a topic that already talked

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.150
about several times on this channel

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.970
and this is slide writing.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:23.500
I want to help you to create
presentations in the style

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.440
of leading consulting firms
like McKinsey, BCG, Bain.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.650
And in today's video, we combine learnings

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.860
from different other videos
that I already created

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.210
on this channel.

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.060
If you've never seen any of these videos

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.510
I will link a playlist
somewhere above here

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:40.960
because today I'm talking
about the creation of charts.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.480
So whenever you will work with data

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.270
in your job, in industry or in consulting,

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.270
charts would always be
a major part of this.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.690
I will show you how to
put together a chart

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.620
that it looks professional,
that it speaks for itself.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.440
So stay tuned and let's dive in.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.940
For the purpose of this video,

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.600
I selected a bit more of a complex chart

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:04.590
and what you can see here
is different countries.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.310
So you can see them as for instance

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:10.440
business units of a company and
all their three data points.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.760
Each of these countries
has three data points

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:14.730
that are depicted here in this chart.

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:18.030
And ed, the example of Germany,
you see that this geography

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.100
for this specific company has
a certain operating margin.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.010
Here's something slightly below eight.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:28.100
It has a CAGR, a compounded
annual growth rate

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.490
of the business of around 6%.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.950
So how much is this business
growing year on year?

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.910
And then there is the market size,

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.680
the market size that is here
now reflected in the diameter

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.200
in the size of this circuit
that is displayed here.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.370
And for charts like this,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.310
for your three dimensions that
you wanna show such a chart

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.740
with an X and a Y axis,

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.690
and then also the size of
the bubbles, for instance

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.250
as third dimension often works quite well.

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.610
Now, in this video, I
wanna put your attention

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.100
to different aspects of
this different things

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.370
that you can now do to
make this chart look like

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.550
it would have been
created by a professional

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:06.040
consulting firm like
McKinsey, BCG, or Bain.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:06.980
Now, for starters

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.870
I'd like to put your
attention on the layout.

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:10.870
So this is a rather clean layout.

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:15.260
Now you could easily make
this layout much more messy.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.190
You could, for instance

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.470
include different colors for the circuits

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.490
include the separator lines

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.500
make this blue, for instance, this red.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.920
So now I introduce different colors

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.510
for the different circuits.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.500
And this is what I often see people do,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.390
color it all up, make it look colorful,

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.690
but this is not what you wanna do.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:36.510
The basic rule is that
whenever you use color,

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.740
color should always have a meaning.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.980
And if it doesn't really portray a message

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.940
you rather go for a basic unified color

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.930
without any differentiation in
the colors that you're using.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:50.880
So if you use color, make
sure to portray meaning by it.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.340
So what could be meaning that
will justify the use of color?

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.120
So if, you know, look at this chart

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.210
one conclusion that you
might be able to draw

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:00.760
from this chart is that
there're actually two regions

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.600
which are extremely interesting

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:03.920
and relevant for this company.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.100
And this would likely be Asia
Pacific and North America.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.200
Because the business in Asia
Pacific has a high margin,

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.550
it is growing extremely
fast and it is rather large.

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:14.830
And regarding North America,

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.800
the numbers are slightly
larger than Asia Pacific,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.570
but the business is even larger,

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.160
making it relevant as well.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.410
Now, if you would want to emphasize this,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.680
this is the message of this chart.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.840
You could now go ahead and for instance

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.520
color these two regions
in blue, just to show

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.180
that this is where the focus
of the audience should be at.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.270
Next thing to look out for are
the legends, the axis labels.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.620
And you see that now we
have really three legends

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.990
or three types of
dimensions we need to label.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:46.260
First, the x-axis, the CAGR,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.340
then Y axis is operating margin.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.010
And then there's additional
information on the diameter.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.430
So again, CAGR stands for
compounded annual growth rate.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.800
Pretty much shows how much this company

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.770
or this business unit, this
region is growing year on year.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.840
And if you want to see
more from me on this topic

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:05.240
because this video is indeed
combining different elements

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.040
of other videos I already created,

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.720
check out my playlist on slide writing.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.530
I will insert a link to the
playlist somewhere above here,

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:16.480
and I even have a full online
course on slide writing.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.100
There will be a link to that included

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.220
in the video description.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:20.660
This is a multi-hour program

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.790
where I teach you all the relevant skills

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.900
and tactics that you need to
create slide presentations

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:27.910
in the style of leading consulting firms.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:29.490
So if this is of interest to you,

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.400
have a look at the link
in the video description.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:32.940
But now if you've seen my other videos,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.240
you know that one of the things

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:35.990
that you should always look out for is

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.500
that whenever you write percentage,

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.400
so pretty much saying

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.660
that this X axis is
showing percentage figures,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:46.520
you should always say the
percentage of what is shown here

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.590
because of course now
the growth rate could be

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.600
the growth rate off all
kinds of different things.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.770
But now, here in this example,

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.070
this is supposed to be the
growth rate of the revenue.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.360
So, let's write percentage of revenue,

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.110
just to make a clear here.

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:02.390
And similar is true for
the operating margin.

00:05:02.390 --> 00:05:05.340
And now you might infer that
this refers to the revenue

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.660
but again, there are also
other margin definitions.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.320
There also things like cost margins.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.150
So here, it's always helpful to, again,

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.950
include percentage of
revenue, just to label margin

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:18.140
of what we are currently looking at.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.050
Now to the diameter of the third dimension

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:20.990
with the market size.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:23.360
Here, you see my suggestion
on how to call this.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.490
Next is something that should be included

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:29.100
on every chart, and this is a chart title.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.630
So I'm just cropping it
over here, a basic element.

00:05:31.630 --> 00:05:34.380
This is pretty much just the
combination of a text box

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.280
and line element that is
just put together here.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.420
Indeed, the chart here

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:42.350
should clearly label what you're seeing

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.930
because this is often quite a struggle.

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.510
You look at a chart like that,
and you're asking yourself,

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:50.470
what exactly am I seeing here?

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.560
So to help your reader
to make this very sane

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:56.990
and transparent, include a
title and clearly label that.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:05:57.823
And for instance,

00:05:57.823 --> 00:06:00.770
here we could write
something like key metrics

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:05.770
for geographical business
units, something like this.

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:07.960
Here again, if you have
another suggestion,

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.580
feel free to let me know in the comments.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:12.180
And indeed, when you write such a title,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:15.400
you can also include other
qualifiers next to it.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.170
So for instance, here it might be relevant

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.970
from what report this is coming,

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:24.970
based on financial reporting, 2014,

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:27.370
something like that is
what you could include

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:29.100
just to make this a really salient.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.870
And then potentially here you could

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.820
also include the year figure.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:33.770
All right, next.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.910
Usually when you create such a chart,

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.870
you do not want to only
leave the viewer alone

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.170
with such a shot, but
you also wanna provide

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.870
some commentary, some implications.

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:44.840
The famous, "So what?"

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.570
You want your chart to have a message.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.080
And of course, if the
reader just looks at this,

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.410
it is really difficult on the first sight,

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.220
get this message out of it.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:56.140
And now the question is,
how do we include this?

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.350
How do we nicely layout this?

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:00.200
And again, if you have
watched other videos

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:03.220
for me on this topic, you
know, one basic principle

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:06.920
of slide writing is
every slide is a table.

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:10.740
Every slide is a table because
it has columns and rows.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.160
So if you look at this page

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:14.250
and you wanna include
additional information,

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.810
think about it as having two columns.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.580
You have now the first
big column to the left,

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.350
where you then include
the chart as we did here.

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.990
And then you can include
a second column here

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:27.780
to the right and I'm
cropping over this element.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.150
And this is now the second column,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.950
where you can now include
some implications.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:36.393
So let's just call it exactly like this.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.710
Let's maybe make this
a little bit smaller,

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:43.270
give a little bit small space to this one.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.040
So now let's copy in a text field.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:49.400
And now indeed, this is
something that we can use just

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.070
to include some commentary
and some remarks.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:52.903
Now, for instance,

00:07:52.903 --> 00:07:56.040
we could write something like Asia Pacific

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.960
and North America, and
then a nice little tweak

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.850
that you can do to emphasize your message

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:05.230
as to bold the most important
points in every bullet.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:07.810
Then it's also nice to color
it, not necessarily in black,

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:09.630
but maybe the main color theme

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.180
of a presentation to make
them really stick out.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:15.350
This often helps to more
easily read these bullets

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:16.990
and get the most important points out.

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:18.370
Let's now finalize this

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:21.160
by creating another key
element of this page.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:23.620
And this is of course, the action title.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:27.110
Here again, I created an
extensive video on action titles.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:28.560
It's in the past on my channel.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.810
I will link it above here, check it out.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.900
So I will not go into too much detail

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.030
on what the action title is all about

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:37.080
and what should be included here.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.900
But for instance, we could write something

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:43.300
like company should further focus

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:47.980
on Asia Pacific and North America

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:52.980
addressing total market
of 428 Euro million.

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:55.090
And again, just to remind you,

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:56.390
it is usually a good idea

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:59.167
whenever you have a page with data on it,

00:08:59.167 --> 00:09:00.930
and of course this would be frequently

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:04.220
the case to then also include
a number in the action title.

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:06.510
This makes it much more
crisp and tangible.

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.510
And this is the feeling
that you wanna go for.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:09.730
I hope this was helpful.

